  abstract = {Identifying latent cluster structures in spatial trends constitutes an important yet challenging task in diverse Received February 2025 applications. In this article, we propose a novel method based on a discrepancy measure over small spatial Accepted December 2025 blocks that effectively uncovers heterogeneity within dynamic spatial data. Our approach effectively detects boundaries where structural changes occur, thus allowing for more nuanced insights into underlying spatial KEYWORDS Boundary detection; patterns. Unlike methods predicated on strong stationarity assumptions, our framework accommodates Functional coefficient; piecewise-defined parameters and irregular sampling locations, enabling its broad applicability to real- Spatial clustering; Temporal world datasets. We further establish asymptotic properties and limit distributions of the proposed methods change points by leveraging the notion of spatial physical dependence, accounting for correlations across spatial domains. Simulations and real data analyses confirm the effectiveness of the method, highlighting its robustness and accuracy in identifying complex spatio-temporal structures. Supplementary materials for this article are available online, including a standardized description of the materials available for reproducing the work.},
